OxiGate's License Agreement

Historically, core network proxy architectures and gateways benefit heavily from
open-source collaboration. To ensure the core remains free and open while allowing
extensibility for proprietary, industrial, or enterprise environments (e.g., custom
codecs, internal protocols, or NDA-encumbered integration modules), OxiGate uses a
dual-licensing structure for core versus exportable interfaces.

Core System & Standard Modules:
All core source code, main binaries, and standard protocol implementations are
licensed under the GNU General Public License, Version 2 (GPL-2.0-only), or later,
unless explicitly stated otherwise in individual file headers.

Exportable Headers & Extensibility Interfaces:
All exportable API headers, include files, and C/Rust binding definitions intended
for external module integration are licensed under the GNU Lesser General Public
License, Version 2.1 (LGPL-2.1-only), or later.

This allows third parties to write, compile, link, and dynamically load external,
binary-only, or proprietary modules using these headers without triggering the copyleft
requirements on the external module itself. However, any modifications or improvements
to the core OxiGate codebase or LGPL header files must remain open source and be
redistributed under their respective licenses upon request.

OpenSSL Exception:
Due to license incompatibilities between the GPL/LGPL and the OpenSSL license,
OxiGate is released with the following explicit exception:

  "Permission is hereby granted to compile, link, and/or execute OxiGate against
   the OpenSSL toolkit (or modified versions thereof that use the same license as
   OpenSSL), and to distribute the resulting executables under the terms of the
   GPL/LGPL as applicable."

The full license texts for GPL-2.0 and LGPL-2.1 are located in the "doc/" directory.